Z||000000||Formula88||02-01-2001||08:52 AM||||I've searched through the past posts, but can't get a good consensus on a clutch for my 88 Formula.  The clutches on clutchnet.com appear to be mainly race-only applications.  I don't want a race clutch because they can be very hard on the rest of the drivetrain.  Someone else said they only got about 45K mi. out of their RAM clutch from the Fiero Store.  I'd like to get more than that.  Anybody have any suggestions for a good, high performance street clutch?  The car is stock (for now) but will be driven hard.  <P>------------------<BR>When you choke a smurf, what color does it turn?||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||02-01-2001||10:40 AM||||here is what George said on the other thread......."Check out my site and scroll down to see the clutch. This is a kevlar clutch specially made for the shop and we carry a double your previously miles warranty, meaning that if you got 45000 miles on your last clutch then the warranty is 90000 miles. One item of importance, if you do not resurface the flywheel then your warranty is null and void. A flywheel can be turned 3 to 4 times before needing replacement. Also a word to the wise. I installed a brand new flywheel and did not resurfaced the unit and ended up pulling the clutch back out to resurface the brand new flywheel. Oh well! live and learn. If you need more info call me. My tel: # is 510-233-4376.  <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.net" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroshop.net</A> <P>Call him and let him read some of the testimonials about this clutch - some even from forum members......... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||02-01-2001||10:49 AM||||Yea, I read about that kevlar clutch, but it's also $375!  I was hoping to get something a little more reasonable in price.  A reman stock clutch kit is about $140 and the Fiero Store's heavy duty RAM clutch kit is ~$210.  Almost twice the price of another brand clutch seems a bit high.  That may be the best, but can anyone suggest something a little more reasonalbe?  Are the RAM clutches good?  I was planning on getting one of them until I read some of the posts about them only lasting 45K miles.  Any testimonials out there?<P>------------------<BR>When you choke a smurf, what color does it turn?||||reg||7
Z||000003||fieroshop||02-01-2001||12:01 PM||||To answer some questions about price  and durability.<BR>Let's say that you bought a clutch for $210.00 and it lasted for 45000 miles or less. At the time you need to replace the clutch again and so now the clutch has cost you 410.00 due to the fact that you had to replace it a lot sooner then my kevlar clutch which is quaranteed for double your previous mile on your clutch. So saying this and you got 45000 miles on your last clutch, then you have a warranty of 90K miles. Not only have you spent $420.00 in parts for 90k miles but you have had to pay for twoo labor changes in your car. Or if you do your own work then you have had replace that clutch twice in 90k miles.<BR>Me I prefer to do the job once and not twice. I have seen my kevlar clutch last as long as three to five times then an ordinary clutch.<BR>Saying this, which clutch ends being cheaper in the long run.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||02-01-2001||12:05 PM||||Thanks for the info.  I'm definately considering your Kevlar clutch.  I do prefer to do it once and do it right.  But since I just bought my Fiero this week - the extra expenditure is a bit much.  I'll check your site for more info on the clutch kit.  What kind of shipping times can I expect?  Also, since I just bought my car, I don't know if it's the original clutch or not.  How would I define my warranty period, then?  The car has 108,000 miles.<P>------------------<BR>When you choke a smurf, what color does it turn?||||reg||6
Z||000005||1FST2M6||02-01-2001||12:16 PM||||Clutchnet kush-loc(disc) and NAPA part # CA1914(pressure plate). you'll be able to drive the car, not shake the dash loose and chirp 4th at 80+ if you want. if your going to use nitrous shy away from the RAM and the CF units i killed both. they can't handle the sudden shock and they will fail. you'll get your $ back from the manufacturer but it's still a pain. the 4 puck i got from ACT is deffinatly a race only disc. it's sucks to drive in traffic.. but it's AWSOME for street racing, or Track/drag racing. tire yanking, head bashing and just BRUTAL! but like i said.. it' lost it's novelty after about a week. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||02-01-2001||01:04 PM||||So the clutchnet Kush-loc is a clutch disc only?  And with the NAPA pressure plate, this should handle heavy duty street use?  Do you think it would handle Nitrous at all?  I want to get as good a clutch as I can afford without going for racing parts.  Also clutchnet lists 2 different clutch disc for an 88 V6 Fiero, 8 7/8 and 9 1/8 O.D. plate.  Any way to tell which one I'll need BEFORE I tear the car down?  Thanks for the help, guys!  Much appreciated!<P>------------------<BR>When you choke a smurf, what color does it turn?||||reg||10
